00:01:39,840 --> 00:01:43,120
This is from Allison, and Alison writes to Holotalk.

24
00:01:43,120 --> 00:01:48,400
She says, I'd love to start doing holograms, but I live in a small apartment.

25
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:50,880
Could you tell me how much space I would need?

26
00:01:50,880 --> 00:01:53,520
And do I need a dark room?

27
00:01:53,520 --> 00:01:55,040
Okay, Allison.

28
00:01:55,040 --> 00:01:59,360
Um space is not too much of a problem with holography.

29
00:01:59,360 --> 00:02:13,160
I remember doing holograms in very small setups, and actually, if you're going to start out doing very simple holograms, you don't need one of the big isolation tables that you see in the labs across the country.

30
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:22,760
Remember, when you're doing a single beam reflection hologram, and that's what most holographers do when they start out doing holography, or just single beam holograms.

31
00:02:22,760 --> 00:02:28,760
And I don't think a lot of people are aware of this, but you do not need to have the laser isolated.

32
00:02:28,760 --> 00:02:34,520
The laser can be sitting on any tabletop or anything that you want to sit it on, just as is.

33
00:02:34,520 --> 00:02:42,520
What you need to have isolated for a single beam reflection hologram is the plate holder and the object being recorded.

34
00:02:42,520 --> 00:02:54,760
So considering the fact that you've got a five or ten milliwatt laser to start out, you're probably going to be working with plate sizes that are about two and a half by two and a half inches or four by five inches.

35
00:02:54,760 --> 00:03:05,160
The only space requirements you would need to do that hologram would be enough space to have that plate holder, however you want to set that up, and the object isolated.

36
00:03:05,160 --> 00:03:08,200
Now there's a few different ways that you can do this.

37
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:19,480
You can get a very small inner tube, not the type of inner tubes from a car, but the type of inner tube that comes from a forklift truck, and you can find them at any industrial supplier.

38
00:03:19,480 --> 00:03:26,440
And they're tiny little tires, they're maybe about a foot or 18 inches in diameter.

39
00:03:26,440 --> 00:03:31,080
And on top of that little tire, you can place a heavy object.

40
00:03:31,080 --> 00:03:33,160
Now there's a couple different ways you can approach this.

41
00:03:33,160 --> 00:03:45,280
You can get a piece of metal, uh metal surface that you can sit down on that that actually has a lot of weight itself, or you can choose something like a large patio block.

42
00:03:44,840 --> 00:03:51,360
I think there's 24 by 24 inch patio blocks that you could sit down on top of that tire table.

43
00:03:51,440 --> 00:03:59,760
This is going to give you your vibration isolation that you're going to need just for the plate holder and the object itself.

44
00:03:59,760 --> 00:04:07,360
On top of that surface, you need to place whatever plate holder you've managed to make for yourself and the object itself.

45
00:04:07,360 --> 00:04:09,600
Make sure that both of these things are stable.

46
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:11,840
That is, you need a nice stable object.

47
00:04:11,840 --> 00:04:20,160
You can't pick something flimsy because even with the best holographic setup, if you've got a flimsy object, you're going to get poor results in the final hologram.

48
00:04:20,160 --> 00:04:23,040
Make sure your object is very stable.

49
00:04:23,040 --> 00:04:25,760
And just go ahead and do your exposure.

50
00:04:25,760 --> 00:04:34,240
Your laser and your beam expanding lens itself does not have to be on that isolation part of the setup.

51
00:04:34,240 --> 00:04:36,800
It can be sitting anywhere that you want it to sit.

52
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:41,280
In fact, if you want to, just do it right down on the floor.

53
00:04:43,600 --> 00:04:48,880
I know a lot of people that use their bathrooms for dark rooms.

54
00:04:48,880 --> 00:04:52,080
This has good and bad things to go along with it.

55
00:04:55,600 --> 00:05:02,640
You don't have to go up or down a flight of steps, say, for instance, you're going to do your development down in the basement of the apartment building or something like that.

56
00:05:02,640 --> 00:05:05,440
You don't have to do that because you have your bathroom right there.

57
00:05:05,440 --> 00:05:09,920
The only thing I get concerned about sometimes is actually ventilation.

58
00:05:09,920 --> 00:05:13,040
Now keep in mind this is not photography.

59
00:05:13,040 --> 00:05:14,640
This is holography.

60
00:05:14,640 --> 00:05:21,200
You're not going to be going into a dark room and making 36 or 72 prints.

61
00:05:21,200 --> 00:05:23,280
You're just not going to do that with holograms.

62
00:05:23,280 --> 00:05:33,000
Usually you'll set up and do one, two, maybe three holograms a night, and you've got time in between those two because you have to go back out of the dark room again to do your next setup.

63
00:05:33,480 --> 00:05:38,840
Now I would suggest that if you have a small window, get a window fan.

64
00:05:38,840 --> 00:05:46,680
And the way that you're going to get around having light come in is I want you to make a hood to go over the outside of that small window fan.

65
00:05:46,680 --> 00:05:50,120
Now we don't need a big window fan, we just need a small one.

66
00:05:50,120 --> 00:05:56,360
And make a hood so that it sort of caps that fan.

67
00:05:56,360 --> 00:06:03,720
And the only way that the air can get out from the fan as an exhaust is through the bottom of that hood.

68
00:06:03,720 --> 00:06:08,520
Now, if you have screens, I would suggest that you double up on the screens.

69
00:06:08,520 --> 00:06:14,680
Take your screens, which are normally a silverish material, and spray paint them a flat black.

70
00:06:14,680 --> 00:06:18,120
And you're going to spray paint two screens.

71
00:06:18,120 --> 00:06:22,280
Put one of the screens in the window and then put the other screen in the window.

72
00:06:22,280 --> 00:06:33,160
And if they're slightly offset, you're not going to see any light coming through because of the obvious small openings in the screen to begin with, along with the black paint and the hood that you have on your fan.

73
00:06:33,160 --> 00:06:46,760
So this will give you a little bit of ventilation, but I don't think you have too much of a problem just being in there to develop a hologram, especially if you're using your traditional type of development chemicals like your D19s and things of that nature.

74
00:06:46,760 --> 00:06:51,240
So, yes, you can do holograms in an apartment very easily.

75
00:06:51,240 --> 00:07:07,000
And if you find that you want to make holograms that are a little bit more complex than your single beam holograms, you can move up to a few of the systems that you can buy that sit on the neoprene isolation balls that you can buy.

76
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:09,080
So there's all kinds of ways of doing it.

77
00:07:09,080 --> 00:07:15,520
You don't have to not do holography just because you live in an apartment.

78
00:07:14,360 --> 00:07:21,440
Our second email question comes from Chris, and Chris writes in, and this is a very interesting question.

79
00:07:21,600 --> 00:07:25,440
I wonder how many holo talk listeners could answer this question.

80
00:07:25,440 --> 00:07:29,600
What was the first hologram made of?

81
00:07:29,600 --> 00:07:39,760
And I'm assuming, Chris, you mean made of as far as an image, the content of the hologram rather than the materials that the hologram was shot on.

82
00:07:40,240 --> 00:07:45,840
The early holograms, even the first hologram was made without using a laser.

83
00:07:45,840 --> 00:07:49,440
A sodium vapor lamp was used to make the first hologram.

84
00:07:49,440 --> 00:07:55,280
Sodium vapor lamps are just like those lamps when you're driving down the street at night, they illuminate the street.

85
00:07:55,280 --> 00:08:09,360
And these lights do not contain the entire range of the visible spectrum as far as wavelengths are concerned, and they can be through careful filtering filtered down to be a monochromatic light source.

86
00:08:09,360 --> 00:08:18,320
However, the problem with using something like that for a hologram is there's another property that's needed to make a hologram, and that is called coherence length.

87
00:08:18,320 --> 00:08:25,680
And it is the coherence length, this property that allows us to record the depth of a hologram.

88
00:08:25,680 --> 00:08:32,720
Now, the coherence length of a sodium vapor lamp is very short, extremely short.

89
00:08:32,720 --> 00:08:38,400
In fact, the first hologram could only record the depth of a postage stamp.

90
00:08:38,400 --> 00:08:47,680
And that first hologram was actually something about the size of a postage stamp, and it had the names of several physicists on it.

91
00:08:47,680 --> 00:09:02,040
So it wasn't actually a true three-dimensional image, although it contained the third dimension enough to show the depth of that piece of paper or postage stamp size material.

92
00:09:02,040 --> 00:09:07,000
But it wasn't anything that you would walk into a museum and say, wow, this is really something.

93
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:09,400
So that's what the first hologram was.

94
00:09:09,400 --> 00:09:20,520
It was made with the sodium vapor lamp and it was a postage size stamp which contained the names of very famous physicists.

